### 1. How to detect

Compare the expected monthly date sequence with the actual staging data. October 2025 was missing for both headline and core CPI.

Then verify whether MoM and YoY calculations use exact calendar dates or row positions. `lag(12)` revealed the issue because a missing row shifted the comparison by one month.

### 2. How to fix

Replace row-based `lag()` calculations with calendar-based self-joins:

* MoM: join to the exact prior month
* YoY: join to the exact month one year earlier

When the required month is missing, return `NULL` instead of calculating from the wrong period.

Apply the same pattern to related monthly models as a defensive fix. Update the formula version when the calculation method changes.

### 3. What I learned

`lag(1)` and `lag(12)` count rows, not calendar periods.

For monthly time-series data, missing dates can silently produce incorrect metrics. Calendar-based joins are safer because they make the intended comparison explicit and fail safely when data is unavailable.
